---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="VPN"
    categorySlug="tools"
    subcategorySlug="vpn"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">The Bali Discovery</h2>
  <p>
    Noticed something weird in Bali last year. Same websites loading way slower than they should, even on decent WiFi.
    Ran some tests and figured out the ISP's DNS servers were crawling. Switched to Cloudflare and pages started loading
    instantly. That twenty-second delay on every site? Gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most people never think about DNS. It's the thing that turns website names into actual addresses your browser can
    find. Your ISP gives you one by default. That default is usually slow, sometimes logs your browsing history, and
    occasionally gets used to redirect you to ads or block content.
  </p>
  <p>
    Changing DNS takes about two minutes. Speed difference can be dramatic. Privacy implications too, which I didn't
    think about until later.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>For those skimming:</strong> Cloudflare if you just want fast. NextDNS when you want network-level ad
    blocking with granular control. AdGuard DNS for families who want content filtering without touching config files.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks</h2>
  <p>Top three DNS services for speed and privacy:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Best DNS services for privacy and speed"
  />
  <p>
    All three are more private than whatever your ISP provides. Pick based on whether you want speed, customization, or simplicity.
  </p>

  <!-- What DNS Actually Does -->
  <h2 id="what-dns-does">What DNS Actually Does</h2>
  <p>
    You type a website address. Your device pings a DNS server asking "hey, where's this thing?" Server shoots back an
    IP address. Browser connects. Page loads.
  </p>
  <p>Your ISP handles this by default. Here's why that's worth caring about:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ISP DNS servers run on aging infrastructure. Millions of queries, not enough hardware upgrades.</li>
    <li>Your ISP sees every domain you request. HTTPS encrypts the page content, but they still know you went there.</li>
    <li>Some ISPs sell this data. Others shove targeted ads at you based on your browsing patterns.</li>
    <li>When governments want browsing records, ISP DNS logs are usually the first stop.</li>
    <li>Failed lookups sometimes redirect to search pages stuffed with ads.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Using different DNS shifts who sees your traffic. Won't make you invisible online. But your ISP stops building a
    map of everywhere you browse.
  </p>

  <!-- Cloudflare -->
  <h2 id="cloudflare">Cloudflare</h2>
  <p>
    Running this on most of my devices. Fastest DNS I've tested across maybe fifteen countries at this point.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare clocked in at 14.52ms average globally when I last checked. That's roughly twice as fast as Google's DNS
    and significantly faster than most ISP defaults. Real world difference: pages feel snappier. The delay between
    clicking a link and something happening shrinks noticeably.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What you get with Cloudflare:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Raw speed that consistently beats everything else</li>
    <li>Privacy commitment—no logging of identifiable data</li>
    <li>Query records kept 25 hours for debugging, then wiped</li>
    <li>APNIC runs independent audits to verify their claims</li>
    <li>Encrypted DNS options available</li>
    <li>Completely free, no usage caps</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Privacy angle is pretty straightforward. Cloudflare made a public commitment to never sell data or target ads. They
    brought in APNIC to audit and confirm they're actually doing what they say. Query data gets purged after 25 hours.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What's missing:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>No content filtering or ad blocking in the standard version</li>
    <li>Can't really customize much</li>
    <li>No dashboard to see your own traffic</li>
    <li>Need their Families variants (1.1.1.2 or 1.1.1.3) for any filtering</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That filtering gap matters if you want ads blocked at the network level. Cloudflare went all-in on speed and keeping
    things dead simple. If you want ads nuked across every device without installing stuff on each one, NextDNS or
    AdGuard handle that better.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare did add two filtered options. 1.1.1.2 catches malware domains. 1.1.1.3 throws in adult content blocking
    for families. Free but zero customization.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Price:</strong> Free. No paid tier for basic DNS.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Works for:</strong> Speed chasers who don't mind handling ad blocking separately. People using browser
    extensions already. Anyone who values minimal setup over tweaking options.
  </p>

  <!-- NextDNS -->
  <h2 id="nextdns">NextDNS</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare wins on speed. NextDNS wins on control. You pick exactly what gets filtered, watch traffic analytics,
    create separate profiles per device.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ran NextDNS for about eight months testing the filtering side. You can block entire ad networks, specific tracking
    domains, malware sites, adult content, gambling sites. The blocklists go deep and stay updated.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What NextDNS packs in:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Build-your-own blocking with stacked filter lists</li>
    <li>Traffic analytics showing blocked requests and sources</li>
    <li>Per-device configuration</li>
    <li>Parental controls with scheduling</li>
    <li>Native apps for everything</li>
    <li>Encrypted DNS baked in</li>
    <li>Logs you own—keep them or delete them entirely</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That analytics dashboard surprised me. After a week on NextDNS, you'll see exactly which apps phone home constantly,
    which sites load tracking garbage, and how much junk your devices were pulling before you started filtering. Found
    one Android app making hundreds of tracking requests daily. Blocked the domain. Done.
  </p>
  <p>
    Setup takes a bit more effort than Cloudflare. Create account, pick your blocklists, install app or punch in DNS
    addresses manually. Maybe ten minutes for basic config.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free tier caps you at 300,000 queries monthly. Sounds generous until you realize a household with phones, tablets,
    smart TVs, and IoT gadgets burns through that fast. After the limit, filtering stops working until next month.
    Queries still resolve, just unfiltered.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The tradeoffs:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Query limits sneak up on you</li>
    <li>Bit slower than Cloudflare, usually 20-40ms</li>
    <li>More knobs means more things to figure out</li>
    <li>You're trusting NextDNS with your logs</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Price:</strong> Free under 300K queries. $1.99/month or $19.90/year removes the cap.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Works for:</strong> Network-wide ad blocking enthusiasts. Parents wanting filtering across every household
    gadget. Privacy folks willing to spend time configuring. People curious about their devices' actual behavior.
  </p>

  <!-- AdGuard DNS -->
  <h2 id="adguard">AdGuard DNS</h2>
  <p>
    AdGuard comes at this differently. It's basically an ad blocker that routes through DNS. Setup couldn't be
    simpler—point devices at their servers and ads start vanishing.
  </p>
  <p>
    No account for basic use. No config required. Swap your DNS to AdGuard's addresses and filtering kicks in
    automatically. They maintain the blocklists and handle the complexity.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What AdGuard brings:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ads and trackers blocked immediately, no setup</li>
    <li>No registration for basic filtering</li>
    <li>Family variant adds content filtering</li>
    <li>Encrypted DNS included</li>
    <li>Open source blocklists anyone can inspect</li>
    <li>Mobile apps for quick setup</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Simplicity is the whole pitch. You want ads gone but don't want to learn DNS internals. No custom filter lists to
    build. Just trust AdGuard's defaults to handle things reasonably.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tested AdGuard's blocking against NextDNS with similar filter lists. Results were close. AdGuard caught most major
    ad networks and trackers. Few things got through that NextDNS would've blocked with its bigger lists, but nothing major.
  </p>
  <p>
    Speed lands between Cloudflare and NextDNS. Response times around 25-45ms typically. You probably won't notice
    unless you're obsessing over latency numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free tier has that same 300,000 query limit. Paid gets you unlimited plus custom rules and detailed stats.
  </p>
  <p>
    Privacy-wise, AdGuard says they store aggregate performance data but skip individual query logging by default.
    They're based in Cyprus, different data laws than the US.
  </p>
  <p><strong>The tradeoffs:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Less granular than NextDNS</li>
    <li>Same 300K monthly limit on free</li>
    <li>Stats require paying</li>
    <li>Slower than Cloudflare</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Price:</strong> Free with query cap. Premium runs about $3/month.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Works for:</strong> Simple ad blocking without learning curves. Families wanting easy content filtering.
    People who don't care about traffic analytics. Set-and-forget types.
  </p>

  <!-- Quad9 -->
  <h2 id="quad9">Quad9</h2>
  <p>
    Including this because it's genuinely different. Quad9 runs as a nonprofit purely focused on security. Malware
    domains, phishing sites, attack infrastructure—blocked. No ad filtering, no content controls. Just threat protection.
  </p>
  <p>
    The nonprofit angle matters for trust. No business model pushing them toward monetizing your data. Funded by IBM,
    Packet Clearing House, and various tech orgs. Headquartered in Switzerland with those famously strong privacy laws.
  </p>
  <p>
    Speed is solid. Not Cloudflare tier but competitive with NextDNS. Security blocking pulls from over twenty threat
    intelligence feeds.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Works for:</strong> Security-first people handling ads separately. Those who like the nonprofit approach.
    Anyone wanting malware protection without ad blocking changing their browsing.
  </p>

  <!-- My Setup -->
  <h2 id="my-setup">What I Actually Use</h2>
  <p>After testing everything, my setup changes by device and location.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Main laptop and phone:</strong> NextDNS with custom blocking. I like seeing what's happening and controlling
    what gets filtered. Paying for unlimited queries.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Home router:</strong> Cloudflare as backup. Whole network gets fast DNS if NextDNS has issues.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Traveling:</strong> Cloudflare on mobile for raw speed. Random airport WiFi already has latency
    problems—faster DNS helps offset that.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Family tablets:</strong> AdGuard DNS. Simple content filtering without managing configs.
  </p>
  <p>Most people can skip this complexity. Just pick based on what matters:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Want maximum speed: Cloudflare</li>
    <li>Want ad blocking with visibility: NextDNS</li>
    <li>Want easy ad blocking, zero config: AdGuard DNS</li>
    <li>Want security from a nonprofit: Quad9</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Setup Guide -->
  <h2 id="setup-guide">Setting Everything Up</h2>
  <p>Takes maybe two minutes per device.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>iPhone:</strong> Go into Wi-Fi settings, tap the little info icon, then Configure DNS and switch to Manual.
    Add the addresses there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Android:</strong> Keeps it under Network & Internet in Private DNS settings. Just type the hostname.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mac:</strong> Hides it in Network preferences under Advanced, then the DNS tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Windows:</strong> Dig through Control Panel to Network Connections, then Properties on your adapter, then
    Internet Protocol Version 4. Punch in the addresses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Router level:</strong> Log into your router admin and hunt for DNS settings. Usually under WAN or Internet.
    Change addresses there and everything on your network uses them automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    NextDNS and AdGuard accounts? Their apps handle config automatically. Install, log in, you're done.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Final Thoughts</h2>
  <p>
    DNS sits in the background affecting every site you visit. Changing it takes a couple minutes and results range from
    subtle improvement to dramatic speedup depending on what you're replacing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare for speed. NextDNS for control. AdGuard for simplicity. All three beat ISP defaults.
  </p>
  <p>
    That Bali situation forced me to actually learn this stuff. Turned a painful slow connection into something workable
    with one settings change. The kind of thing you set up once and forget about.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8">
    <a
      href="https://nordvpn.com/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 bg-jet-stream text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-jet-stream/90 transition-colors"
    >
      Get NordVPN - Complete Privacy Protection
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- Full Width Content Slot -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <!-- Full Comparison Table -->
    <section class="py-8" id="full-comparison">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 md:px-6">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-6 text-center font-heading">Complete DNS Service Comparison</h2>
        <p class="text-white/80 text-center mb-8 max-w-2xl mx-auto">Here's how all the DNS services compare on the
          features that matter most for privacy and speed:</p>
        <ComparisonTable
            columns={fullComparisonColumns}
            rows={fullComparisonRows}
            caption="Complete comparison of DNS services for privacy and speed"
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Related Articles -->
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
